I was watching an interview of Richard Stallman with a journalist that
turned into a heated debate about Free vs Proprietary software which
nsadly is not the topic of the current text.  Richard also mentioned
Free vs Open Source Software and how they are nothing
alike.  In order to understand where the distinction lies, we first
need to clarify  the definitions.

**So what is a free software?**

According to [GNU](https://www.gnu.org/philosophy/free-sw.html) a software is considered to be free when it satisfies
the four essential freedoms:

1.  The freedom to run the program for any purpose
2.  The freedom to modify the program as you wish
3.  The freedom to redistribute copies of the program
4.  The freedom to distribute copies of a modified version of the
    program

By definition a software that follows those essential freedoms must
have its source code openly available viz it has to be an `open source
software` .  Any license that follows those guidelines is considered
to fall under `Free Software's` umbrella.  The main representative of
`Free Software` that encapsulates its philosophy and its general
essence is the `GPL` license which comes with an additional caveat;
Any modification of the software must come with `GPL` license as well.

**What is an open source software?**

Firstly, we need to distinguish the meaning of the expression `open
source software` from the meaning of the _de facto_ definition of
`Open Source Software`, which we will denote by capitalizing the first
letter of each word.

-   `open source software`
    The obvious meaning of this expression is that people can look at the

source code of the program.  Most people seem to misunderstand open
source software in this narrowly defined sense which has nothing to do
with the strict rule-based efinition of Free Software.

-   `Open Source Software` (OSS)
    is a looser version of Free Software.

The official definition, as described by [OSI](https://opensource.org/osd) (which is recognized by several countries as the
_de facto_ OSS definition), is almost the same as Free Software
definition.  It's source code is openly available, it can be
distributed, it can be modified and the modified versions can also be
distributed.

**So what's the difference between the two?**

The main difference lies in the general philosophy of both terms.  On
one hand Free Software is focused on users' rights and a strict
ideological approach to the matter while OSS takes a more neutral and
pragmatic stance.  We can understand  their approaches better if we take
a glimpse at their main license representatives, GPL for Free
Software and MIT for OSS.

-   The GPL license
    In addition to the four main rules of the Free Software the GPL
    license has a `copyleft` rule.  Copyleft states that any organization
    or individual that has modified the source code of a software under
    the GPL license, _if_ she decides to ever release the program it is
    **required** to also release the modified source code.  A notable
    example of a software under GPL license is the Android Open Source
    Project which is a modified version of the Linux kernel.
-   MIT license MIT license has no such restrictions, leaving the user
    or the organization free to decide whether they release the modified
    source code of a software under MIT license.  This means that any
    organization can modify an _open source_ software licensed under
    MIT, modify it, and then release a proprietary version of it without
    being obliged to release the source code.  An example of a OSS under
    MIT license that has gone proprietary is Google Chrome which is a
    modified version of the Chromium Project's browser.

If someone considers GPL as the license of his software then he
ensures that the software will remain free.  This might cause
hesitation to developers that might want to incorporate the software
to an existing proprietary base, eventually choosing a more permissive
license such as MIT.  I like to picture those licenses as two trees
growing side by side.  The MIT tree is dense on it's base - it is
preferred by many developers due to its permissiveness -and short- it
is easier for a branch to go proprietary and eventually halt its
development.  On the other hand the GPL tree is sparser on its base
-its restrictive nature doesn't make it a popular choice across
developers- but taller -the mandatory freedom to propagate the
existing branches make it less likely for its development to halt,
leading to bigger heights than its rival.
